  "question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThe project involves the development of a Django-based system to provide group account functionality, enhancing the management of user access and subscriptions within an application. This framework, Django Organizations, allows users to manage and participate in multi-user groups independent of individual user identities while integrating seamlessly with existing user models.\n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary objective is to integrate multi-user organizational structures into Django applications. This includes functionality for user invitation, registration, and message handling, with a strong emphasis on backend and authentication agnosticism.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Implement Django Organizations into their projects to enhance user and account management.\n- End Users: Individuals and administrators who manage or partake in organizations as members or owners.\n- Organizations: Entities using the system to manage collective accounts, subscriptions, and roles.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Group Account Management: Users can create and manage multiple organizations, with detailed role assignments and permissions.\n- Multi-user Participation: Users can be members or owners of multiple organizations concurrently.\n- Identity Separation: Individual user identity is distinct from organizational roles, facilitating easier management.\n- Invitation and Registration: Built-in functionalities for inviting users and handling registrations, with extensibility options.\n- Customizability: Developers can start with base models or customize as needed for specific project requirements.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Backend Agnostic: Compatible with various backend systems, requiring minimal adaptation.\n- Authentication Agnostic: Works with Django's own authentication system or any custom implementations.\n- Scalability: Designed to support multiple organizations and high user concurrency.\n- Extensibility: Easily extendable to introduce additional features or modify existing behaviors as project needs evolve.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0075\nCREATE TABLE `organization` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `organization_slug_uniq` (`slug`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `organization_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `organization_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `is_owner` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `joined_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `organization_user_uniq` (`organization_id`,`user_id`),\n  KEY `organization_user_ibfk_2` (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `organization_user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`organization_id`) REFERENCES `organization` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `organization_user_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `organization_invitation` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `organization_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `invited_by_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `token` char(32) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `accepted_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `organization_invitation_token_uniq` (`token`),\n  KEY `organization_invitation_ibfk_2` (`invited_by_id`),\n  KEY `organization_invitation_ibfk_1` (`organization_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `organization_invitation_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`organization_id`) REFERENCES `organization` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `organization_invitation_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`invited_by_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `organization_message` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `organization_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `sender_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `subject` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `body` text NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `organization_message_ibfk_1` (`organization_id`),\n  KEY `organization_message_ibfk_2` (`sender_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `organization_message_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`organization_id`) REFERENCES `organization` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `organization_message_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`sender_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
